## Limitations

- A finding proves that published values cannot all be right. It does not say which value is wrong or why.
- Cohort sizes, sample counts and data coverage are not published, so differences between cohorts cannot be tested for
  significance. The guardrail says so instead of guessing.
- OAH profile checks are transcribed by hand from the IG's FSH (the sandbox does not host the profiles and the IG website was
  offline), and cover the checkable subset of constraints.
- Plausibility "typical ranges" are general; values outside them are reported as unusual, never as wrong.
- Data you bring is judged with OneAquaHealth knowledge: statistical and structural rules apply to any FHIR data, range checks
  only to measures Data Doctor knows. Auditing a whole other server works for servers of this size; very large servers would need
  search filters, which are not implemented.
- The analysis catalog is small and curated, so the impact trace reports only what Data Doctor computes, not every possible use.
- Some thresholds are judgement, not universal scientific truth: the 100× mean/median ratio (SEM-SCALE-001), the 10 %
  PM2.5/PM10 tolerance that separates error from warning (SEM-XREC-001), the series-break factors (SEM-TEMP-001) and the typical
  ranges. Each rule states its confidence and rationale (docs/04_RULES_CATALOG.md).
- The live sandbox is shared and writable, so live results can change between runs; each run records its time and hashes, and
  the snapshot reproduces the published numbers.
- A comparability verdict covers the eight checked dimensions; a comparison may still need contextual scientific review (for
  example, data coverage is not published). Claim verdicts are bounded by the evidence in the data: no individual-level data
  and no published uncertainty.
- A language-model explanation, when switched on, never determines a finding or a verdict.
- The IG source declares no licence at the pinned commit. Derived knowledge is attributed and kept separate (see NOTICE).

## AI policy and AI assistance disclosure

In the product: detection, comparability and claim verdicts are deterministic code; the product is complete with
`DD_LLM_PROVIDER=none` (the default). An optional language model may only reword a computed finding (numbers, causes and
corrections it adds are detected and the text discarded) or fill gaps in the keyword reading of a typed claim. Details:
docs/AI_POLICY.md.


This project was built with substantial help from an AI coding assistant (Claude, by Anthropic), which drafted code, tests and
documentation under human direction. The product itself uses no AI to detect problems or decide verdicts. An optional,
switched-off-by-default language model (free local Ollama, any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, or Claude) can only rephrase already
computed findings or fill gaps in the keyword reading of a typed claim. The demo video's narration is synthetic speech
(ElevenLabs text to speech), reading a script we wrote and checked against the app.

